;; Docs for std/syntax-match.bjo, read through (:doc #:file "syntax-match.bjodoc").
;; The texts are samizdat markup: code in prose is @code{...}.

(:doc #:module
  (summary "Pattern matching on syntax, to write macros with.")
  (see syntax-match all-of)
  (example """
    (import (std syntax-match))

    (def/macro (my-let form inject compare)
      (syntax-match form
        ((_ ((name value) ...) body ...)
         #'((fun (,@name) ,@body) ,@value))
        (bad (syntax-error bad "my-let takes a binding list and a body."))))
    """)
  (reference """
    @code{#'} builds a form and @code{syntax-match} takes one apart. Write a
    macro transformer with it, not with a @code{match} on @code{Syntax}.

    A @code{syntax-match} expands to one @code{match}. A pattern becomes the
    pattern of an arm. A test that a shape cannot express becomes the
    @code{#:when} of the arm. An ellipsis over a compound pattern becomes a
    fold through @code{all-of}, which this module exports for that reason.
    See @code{Docs/MACROS.org}, "syntax-match takes one apart".
    """))

(:doc all-of
  (summary "Applies f to each item, and answers all the results, or None if f answers None for one.")
  (arg items "The items.")
  (arg f "The function to apply. It answers @code{None} to reject an item.")
  (returns "@code{(Some results)} in the order of @code{items} when f accepts every item, else @code{None}.")
  (see syntax-match)
  (example """
    (all-of (list 1 2 3) (fun (n) (if (> n 0) (Some (* n 2)) None)))   ; (Some (list 2 4 6))
    (all-of (list 1 -2 3) (fun (n) (if (> n 0) (Some n) None)))        ; None
    """)
  (reference """
    The expansion of @code{syntax-match} calls it for an ellipsis over a
    compound pattern, such as @code{((name value) ...)}. It stops at the
    first item that f rejects. On an empty list it answers
    @code{(Some (list))}.
    """))

(:doc syntax-match
  (summary "Matches a piece of syntax against patterns, and runs the body of the first clause that matches.")
  (form (syntax-match subject clause ...))
  (arg subject "An expression that evaluates to @code{Syntax}, usually the form a macro was given.")
  (rest clause """
    @code{(pattern body ...)}. At least one clause. Each clause needs at
    least one body form. The variables of the pattern are in scope in the
    body, and the value of the last body form is the value of the
    @code{syntax-match}.
    """)
  (see all-of)
  (example """
    (def/macro (swap! form inject compare)
      (syntax-match form
        ((_ a b)
         #'(let ((tmp ,a)) (set! ,a ,b) (set! ,b tmp)))
        (bad (syntax-error bad "swap! takes two variables: (swap! a b)."))))

    ;; 'else matches the identifier else; a keyword matches that keyword
    (syntax-match clause
      (('else body ...) #'(begin ,@body))
      ((#:when test body ...) #'(when ,test ,@body))
      ((test body ...) #'(if ,test (begin ,@body) unit)))
    """)
  (reference """
    A pattern is one of these:

    @code{_} matches anything and binds nothing.

    @code{name} matches anything and binds it to @code{name}. A variable is
    lowercase. An uppercase name is an error, because it looks like a union
    case: write @code{'Cons} to match the identifier @code{Cons}.

    @code{'name} matches the identifier @code{name}. It compares with
    @code{syntax-ident=?}, so hygiene marks are ignored.

    An integer, a string, a character or a keyword matches the same literal.

    @code{(p q)} matches a form with exactly that many items, each against
    its pattern.

    @code{(p q ...)} matches a form with at least the items before
    @code{q}, and each remaining item must match @code{q}. When @code{q} is
    a variable, it binds the remaining items as a @code{(List Syntax)}.
    When @code{q} is a compound pattern, each variable in it binds a list of
    what it matched, one entry for each item. Ellipses nest:
    @code{((head arg ...) ...)} binds @code{head} to a list and @code{arg}
    to a list of lists.

    A form can have only one @code{...}, and it must be the last item. A
    pattern must not bind the same name two times. These are syntax errors
    at the pattern.

    The clauses are tried in order. When a clause does not match, the next
    clause is tried. When the pattern of the last clause is a variable or
    @code{_}, it matches everything. Otherwise, @code{syntax-match} adds an
    arm that answers a syntax error, "No clause of this syntax-match
    matched.", at the subject. Write a last clause such as
    @code{(bad (syntax-error bad "..."))} to give a message that names the
    macro.
    """))
